# JavaScript Code to Remove "(age X)" Pattern

Here's a JavaScript code that removes the `(age X)` pattern where `X` can be any number:

```javascript
const str = "july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)";

const result = str.replace(/\(age\s*\d+\)\s*/g, '');

console.log(result);
// Output: "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"
```

## Explanation

| Regex Part | Meaning |
|------------|---------|
| `\(` | Matches the literal opening parenthesis `(` |
| `age` | Matches the literal text "age" |
| `\s*` | Matches zero or more whitespace characters |
| `\d+` | Matches one or more digits (handles any number like 7, 29, 100, etc.) |
| `\)` | Matches the literal closing parenthesis `)` |
| `\s*` | Matches any trailing whitespace after the pattern |
| `g` | Global flag — removes all occurrences if there are multiple |

## More Examples

```javascript
// Works with any age number
"july 7 1994 (age 29) London, UK (1994-07-07)".replace(/\(age\s*\d+\)\s*/g, '');
// → "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"

"july 7 1994 (age 100) London, UK (1994-07-07)".replace(/\(age\s*\d+\)\s*/g, '');
// → "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"

"july 7 1994 (age 3) London, UK (1994-07-07)".replace(/\(age\s*\d+\)\s*/g, '');
// → "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"
```

The key here is that `\d+` matches **any number** (one or more digits), so it will work regardless of what the age value is.

